<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Liberation Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's Liberation Army is the armed force of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, tracing its origin to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanchang">Nanchang</a> uprising of 1927. Having won the civil war against the Nationalists, it extended the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> in 1949 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and the borderlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in 1950 and 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army.jpg" alt="People's Liberation Army" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 燃灯 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_Emblem_PLA_shaded.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The People's Liberation Army (PLA) traces its origins to the left-wing units of the National Revolutionary Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>, which broke away in 1927 in an uprising against the nationalist government as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-red-army">Chinese Red Army</a>. In 1925 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> (CCP) had renamed its Central Military Department the Central Military Commission; it was first led by Zhang Guotao and from 1926 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a>. During the First United Front it was less a unified command than an administrative liaison with other armed communist groups.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> the communist units were reintegrated into the National Revolutionary Army as the New Fourth Army and the Eighth Route Army. The two were reconstituted as the PLA in 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the People's Republic was established in 1949, the army was reorganised. An Air Force leadership structure was set up in November 1949 and a Navy structure the following April. The early PLA consisted overwhelmingly of peasants and treated soldiers and officers in an egalitarian way; formal ranks were not adopted until 1955. The sociologist Alessandro Russo describes this as a break with traditional Chinese hierarchies. The first Military Service Law of 1955 created compulsory service.</p><p>Its engagements include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a>, the Second Sino-Japanese War, the incorporation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> in 1949 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in 1950, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> under the banner of the Chinese People's Volunteer Army (1950–1953), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan-strait">Taiwan Strait</a> crises of 1954–1955 and 1958, the Sino-Indian War of 1962, the Sino-Soviet border conflict of 1969 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-vietnamese-war">Sino-Vietnamese War</a> of 1979. It has also sent units, often engineers and logistics troops, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> peacekeeping missions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The PLA is the armed wing of the CCP, which holds that the party commands the gun. It is governed by the Central Military Commission, which exists as a party body and a state body with identical membership. Regiments, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> and larger units have party committees and political commissars, who are officially equal to commanders in status. Operational control is divided between the service headquarters and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> commands, grouped by geography.</p><p>The PLA now has four services, the Ground Force, Navy, Air Force and Rocket Force, and four arms established on 19 April 2024. Women took part extensively in combat during the revolutionary period and the civil war; after 1949 their role shrank mostly to support, medical and logistics duties.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's Liberation Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1945 to 1948 Manchuria was a base of the <b>People's Liberation Army</b> against the Nationalists, many former Manchukuo soldiers joining the Communists because the Nationalists executed collaborators.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…1964 stage work The East Is Red, was played as China's team entered the 1988 Seoul Olympics, was heard in the 1993 film Farewell My Concubine and was played by the band of the <b>People's Liberation Army</b> before the ceremony of the handover of Hong Kong in 1997.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ode-to-the-motherland">Ode to the Motherland</a></li><li><blockquote>The remaining leadership, with Saifuddin Azizi, accepted the <b>People's Liberation Army</b>, and the region became part of the People's Republic of China.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-east-turkestan-republic">Second East Turkestan Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Qing and Republican governments increasingly intervened, and the <b>People's Liberation Army</b> took control in 1950.</blockquote><a href="/state/sipsong-panna">Sipsong Panna</a></li><li><blockquote>In October 1950 the <b>People's Liberation Army</b> defeated the Tibetan army at Chamdo.</blockquote><a href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951)</a></li></ul></section>
